<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Via Salaria</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Salt Road</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Via Salaria was an ancient road running north-east from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> through Sabine territory and across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> to the Adriatic coast near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castrum">Castrum</a> Truentinum. Its name, the salt road, comes from its early use for carrying salt from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> mouth to the Sabines, and it is among the oldest routes of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Fidenae, just north of Rome, owed its importance to its position on the road and the Tiber.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/via-salaria.jpg" alt="Via Salaria" width="320" height="282" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NielsF · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Roman_roads_in_Italy.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
